1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,400
{\an8}THE LONG MEADOW

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:06,803
[water, moving somewhere out of shot]

3
00:00:07,703 --> 00:00:11,203
This field has been under water on purpose

4
00:00:11,523 --> 00:00:14,606
every winter for three hundred years.

5
00:00:14,926 --> 00:00:18,426
Not flooded. Flooding is what a river does

6
00:00:18,746 --> 00:00:21,413
when nobody is paying attention.

7
00:00:21,733 --> 00:00:25,150
This is drowning, and it is done by hand.

8
00:00:25,470 --> 00:00:28,970
<i>A man in waders walks a line in the grass.</i>

9
00:00:29,290 --> 00:00:32,623
The man is a drowner. That is his trade.

10
00:00:32,943 --> 00:00:36,193
Not a thing that happens to him. A job.

11
00:00:36,513 --> 00:00:40,180
- Say it again for the microphone.
- Drowner.

12
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:43,583
- And your father?
- Drowner. And his.

13
00:00:43,903 --> 00:00:47,070
This is Winifred Sallis, of Wraycombe.

14
00:00:47,390 --> 00:00:51,140
Her family has drowned
this meadow since 1847.

15
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:55,043
The water comes off the
Ashe, a chalk river,

16
00:00:55,363 --> 00:00:58,446
which in January runs at ten degrees.

17
00:00:58,766 --> 00:01:01,766
The air, this morning, is minus two.

18
00:01:02,086 --> 00:01:05,503
That difference is the whole of the idea.

19
00:01:05,823 --> 00:01:09,490
<i>Steam lifting off the
surface at first light.</i>

20
00:01:09,810 --> 00:01:12,227
The water has to keep moving.

21
00:01:12,547 --> 00:01:15,880
Still water freezes and kills the grass.

22
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:20,117
Moving water carries its
warmth across the field

23
00:01:20,437 --> 00:01:24,104
and holds the frost an
inch above the ground.

24
00:01:24,424 --> 00:01:28,674
- So the grass never knows it is winter.
- It knows.

25
00:01:28,994 --> 00:01:32,327
- But it grows anyway?
- It grows anyway.

26
00:01:32,647 --> 00:01:36,397
Grass here starts three
weeks before the hill.

27
00:01:36,717 --> 00:01:40,050
Three weeks, in 1847, was the difference

28
00:01:40,370 --> 00:01:43,620
between selling lambs and burying them.

29
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:47,273
[a hatch board dragged up in its groove]

30
00:01:48,173 --> 00:01:51,756
The machinery is older
than the machine age.

31
00:01:52,076 --> 00:01:55,493
A hatch is a board in a groove in a wall.

32
00:01:55,813 --> 00:01:59,313
Lift it, and the river
goes where you want.

33
00:01:59,633 --> 00:02:03,050
Drop it, and the river forgets the field.

34
00:02:03,370 --> 00:02:07,037
There is no other
mechanism. There never was.

35
00:02:07,357 --> 00:02:11,274
<i>Pennyquick Hatch, rebuilt
1923, still hung true.</i>

36
00:02:11,594 --> 00:02:14,927
From the hatch, the water enters a main.

37
00:02:15,247 --> 00:02:18,914
A main is not a pipe.
It is a raised channel,

38
00:02:19,234 --> 00:02:22,734
cut and banked so it runs above the field.

39
00:02:26,934 --> 00:02:30,267
From the main it goes into the carriers.

40
00:02:30,587 --> 00:02:34,587
A carrier carries water
on. A drain takes it off.

41
00:02:34,907 --> 00:02:39,157
Between them, six inches
of fall in a hundred yards.

42
00:02:39,477 --> 00:02:42,894
- Six inches.
- Six. Over a hundred yards.

43
00:02:43,214 --> 00:02:47,131
- Measured how?
- By eye, mostly. Then by grass.

44
00:02:47,451 --> 00:02:50,868
The grass tells you where you were wrong.

45
00:02:51,188 --> 00:02:54,355
A pale patch is a place the water sat.

46
00:02:54,675 --> 00:02:58,092
A dark patch is a place it never reached.

47
00:02:58,412 --> 00:03:02,412
<i>Aerial: the field is a
page of faint ruled lines.</i>

48
00:03:02,732 --> 00:03:07,315
Those lines are not ploughing.
Nothing is ploughed here.

49
00:03:07,635 --> 00:03:11,302
They are carriers, cut
by hand, kept by hand,

50
00:03:11,622 --> 00:03:15,705
and recut every autumn
since the reign of William.

51
00:03:16,025 --> 00:03:19,608
The work has a name.
It is called stitching.

52
00:03:19,928 --> 00:03:24,428
- Because it looks like sewing?
- Because it is sewing.

53
00:03:24,748 --> 00:03:28,998
You open the ground, you
lay the line, you close it.

54
00:03:29,318 --> 00:03:33,401
A drowner stitches about
a mile of channel a year.

55
00:03:33,721 --> 00:03:36,971
Winifred Sallis is sixty-one years old.

56
00:03:37,291 --> 00:03:41,541
She has stitched, by her
own reckoning, forty miles.

57
00:03:41,861 --> 00:03:45,028
[a spade going into wet ground, twice]

58
00:03:45,928 --> 00:03:49,261
The season begins at the end of October.

59
00:03:49,581 --> 00:03:52,581
The hatches come up. The mains fill.

60
00:03:52,901 --> 00:03:56,401
For four days the field is a moving sheet.

61
00:03:56,721 --> 00:04:00,304
That sheet has a name
of its own. The float.

62
00:04:00,624 --> 00:04:04,541
- How deep is a float?
- Two inches. Never more.

63
00:04:04,861 --> 00:04:09,611
- And if it is more?
- Then it is a flood, and it is mine.

64
00:04:09,931 --> 00:04:13,764
Two inches of water,
moving, over twenty acres.

65
00:04:14,084 --> 00:04:17,917
Then the hatches drop
and the field drains dry,

66
00:04:18,237 --> 00:04:22,487
and a fortnight later the
whole thing is done again.

67
00:04:22,807 --> 00:04:26,140
Five times, six times, through to March.

68
00:04:26,460 --> 00:04:30,627
- How do you know when to float?
- Not by the book.

69
00:04:30,947 --> 00:04:34,280
There is a book. It was printed in 1801.

70
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:38,100
It gives the dates,
and the dates are wrong

71
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:41,670
in about two winters out of every five.

72
00:04:41,990 --> 00:04:44,990
- Then what do you use?
- The willow.

73
00:04:45,310 --> 00:04:48,560
Willow on the bank breaks bud too early

74
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:52,130
in a winter that is going to turn mild.

75
00:04:52,450 --> 00:04:56,033
- And that has held?
- Better than the book.

76
00:05:00,233 --> 00:05:04,733
<i>A willow, bare, photographed
on the same day each year.</i>

77
00:05:05,053 --> 00:05:09,886
Alban Pryce has the photographs,
and the book, and a graph.

78
00:05:10,206 --> 00:05:14,039
<i>Archive, 1958: three men
and a boy on the bank.</i>

79
00:05:14,359 --> 00:05:17,942
This is the meadow under
the last full crew.

80
00:05:18,262 --> 00:05:22,095
Four men, twenty acres,
and no engine anywhere.

81
00:05:22,415 --> 00:05:25,415
The boy is Tobias Reed, aged eleven.

82
00:05:25,735 --> 00:05:29,318
He is now the last
drowner on the upper Ashe

83
00:05:29,638 --> 00:05:33,138
apart from the woman you have already met.

84
00:05:33,458 --> 00:05:37,125
- They gave you a spade at eleven?
- At nine.

85
00:05:37,445 --> 00:05:41,445
- And what did you cut?
- Nothing. I carried tea.

86
00:05:41,765 --> 00:05:44,932
The tea was carried a mile and a half.

87
00:05:45,252 --> 00:05:49,002
By the time it arrived
it was a rumour of tea.

88
00:05:49,322 --> 00:05:51,739
The water has to keep moving.

89
00:05:52,059 --> 00:05:55,559
So does the man. There
is no standing about

90
00:05:55,879 --> 00:05:59,379
in a field that is two inches under water.

91
00:05:59,699 --> 00:06:03,949
<i>A carrier, close: the surface
is not still anywhere.</i>

92
00:06:04,269 --> 00:06:07,769
What the film cannot
show you is the sound.

93
00:06:08,089 --> 00:06:11,922
[the meadow, recorded
flat, for eleven seconds]

94
00:06:12,822 --> 00:06:16,239
Twenty acres of moving water is not loud.

95
00:06:16,559 --> 00:06:19,976
It is a hiss, about as loud as breathing.

96
00:06:20,296 --> 00:06:24,046
Tobias Reed says he can
hear a blocked carrier

97
00:06:24,366 --> 00:06:28,283
from the gate, which is
four hundred yards away.

98
00:06:28,603 --> 00:06:32,936
- That cannot be true.
- Come at four in the morning.

99
00:06:33,256 --> 00:06:35,839
We came at four in the morning.

100
00:06:36,159 --> 00:06:40,076
[the same field, and one
small wrong note in it]

101
00:06:40,976 --> 00:06:44,393
There was a dead branch across a carrier,

102
00:06:44,713 --> 00:06:47,546
and he had heard it from the gate.

103
00:06:47,866 --> 00:06:51,449
<i>He lifts it out without
breaking his stride.</i>

104
00:06:51,769 --> 00:06:55,019
The first written record of this meadow

105
00:06:55,339 --> 00:06:58,589
is a bill for hatch boards, dated 1698.

106
00:06:58,909 --> 00:07:02,492
Eleven shillings, to a
carpenter named Fell.

107
00:07:02,812 --> 00:07:06,312
The bill survives because it was disputed.

108
00:07:06,632 --> 00:07:09,715
- Disputed for how long?
- Nine years.

109
00:07:10,035 --> 00:07:14,618
This is Marguerite Okonkwo,
who wrote the county survey.

110
00:07:14,938 --> 00:07:18,521
She has read every water
dispute on the Ashe

111
00:07:18,841 --> 00:07:22,258
between 1690 and the end of the last war.

112
00:07:22,578 --> 00:07:26,245
- How many are there?
- Four hundred and six.

113
00:07:30,445 --> 00:07:33,362
- All about water?
- All about when.

114
00:07:33,682 --> 00:07:37,515
Water is not the scarce
thing on a chalk river.

115
00:07:37,835 --> 00:07:41,335
Water is endless. What is scarce is order.

116
00:07:41,655 --> 00:07:45,155
If the meadow above you floats on Tuesday,

117
00:07:45,475 --> 00:07:49,808
you cannot float until
Thursday, and you will say so.

118
00:07:50,128 --> 00:07:53,961
<i>A page of court roll,
close: a column of names.</i>

119
00:07:54,281 --> 00:07:57,614
The dates on that page are the argument.

120
00:07:57,934 --> 00:08:01,684
Every one of those names
wanted the same week.

121
00:08:02,004 --> 00:08:05,421
The settlement, when it came, was a rota.

122
00:08:05,741 --> 00:08:09,408
Not a law. A rota, agreed
in a room, in 1712.

123
00:08:09,728 --> 00:08:13,561
- And it held?
- It held for two hundred years.

124
00:08:13,881 --> 00:08:19,131
- Why did it hold?
- Because everyone was downstream of someone.

125
00:08:19,451 --> 00:08:23,201
[church bell, distant,
striking the half hour]

126
00:08:24,101 --> 00:08:27,684
The rota is still pinned
in the parish room.

127
00:08:28,004 --> 00:08:31,587
Four names on it now.
There were thirty-one.

128
00:08:31,907 --> 00:08:36,074
<i>The list, photographed in
1904 and again this year.</i>

129
00:08:36,394 --> 00:08:40,227
The meadow reached its
greatest extent in 1847,

130
00:08:40,547 --> 00:08:44,547
the year the Sallis family
arrived from the west.

131
00:08:44,867 --> 00:08:48,617
Two thousand acres of
the valley were drowned.

132
00:08:48,937 --> 00:08:51,354
The water has to keep moving.

133
00:08:51,674 --> 00:08:55,757
And it did, through four
hundred miles of channel,

134
00:08:56,077 --> 00:08:59,160
every yard of it opened with a spade.

135
00:08:59,480 --> 00:09:03,147
- Four hundred miles.
- In this valley alone.

136
00:09:03,467 --> 00:09:06,967
The men who cut it were paid by the chain.

137
00:09:07,287 --> 00:09:11,287
A chain is twenty-two
yards, and the rate in 1847

138
00:09:11,607 --> 00:09:15,607
was fourpence, which is
a long way for fourpence.

139
00:09:15,927 --> 00:09:19,510
<i>A ledger: names, chains,
pence, in one hand.</i>

140
00:09:19,830 --> 00:09:23,163
The same ledger records what it was for.

141
00:09:23,483 --> 00:09:26,233
Hay. Not grass, not grazing. Hay.

142
00:09:26,553 --> 00:09:29,553
An early bite for the ewes in March,

143
00:09:29,873 --> 00:09:33,956
and then the field shut
up for a hay crop in June.

144
00:09:34,276 --> 00:09:37,693
Two harvests from one field, in a country

145
00:09:38,013 --> 00:09:41,596
where one harvest was
the usual arrangement.

146
00:09:41,916 --> 00:09:46,416
- So it paid for itself.
- It paid for the church roof.

147
00:09:46,736 --> 00:09:50,653
It also paid for the
school, and the almshouses,

148
00:09:50,973 --> 00:09:54,723
and a lawsuit in 1802
that nobody now defends.

149
00:09:55,043 --> 00:09:58,210
[a scythe, being sharpened, unhurried]

150
00:10:02,410 --> 00:10:05,993
The hay came off in the
second week of June.

151
00:10:06,313 --> 00:10:09,646
Forty people in a field for eleven days.

152
00:10:09,966 --> 00:10:14,049
<i>Archive, 1931: a line of
scythes, moving together.</i>

153
00:10:14,369 --> 00:10:17,869
Watch the line rather
than any one of them.

154
00:10:18,189 --> 00:10:21,689
They are not racing.
They are keeping step.

155
00:10:22,009 --> 00:10:26,092
A man who goes fast in a
scythe line is a nuisance

156
00:10:26,412 --> 00:10:29,912
and will be told so at the end of the row.

157
00:10:30,232 --> 00:10:34,149
- Were you ever told so?
- Once. I was fourteen.

158
00:10:34,469 --> 00:10:37,719
- And?
- And I have not been fast since.

159
00:10:38,039 --> 00:10:41,289
The last hay crop taken off this meadow

160
00:10:41,609 --> 00:10:44,526
by hand, with scythes, was in 1948.

161
00:10:44,846 --> 00:10:48,263
The last taken off it at all was in 1971.

162
00:10:48,583 --> 00:10:52,333
<i>The field, that summer,
from the church tower.</i>

163
00:10:52,653 --> 00:10:55,486
What ended it was not the tractor.

164
00:10:55,806 --> 00:10:59,223
The tractor could have worked this field.

165
00:10:59,543 --> 00:11:02,543
What ended it was a bag of nitrogen.

166
00:11:02,863 --> 00:11:06,530
Fertiliser does in a
fortnight, on any field,

167
00:11:06,850 --> 00:11:09,850
what the river did here in a winter.

168
00:11:10,170 --> 00:11:14,587
- And cheaper?
- Far cheaper. That is the whole of it.

169
00:11:14,907 --> 00:11:18,657
The hatches at Pennyquick
were dropped in 1971

170
00:11:18,977 --> 00:11:22,477
and not lifted again
for thirty-four years.

171
00:11:22,797 --> 00:11:26,130
[silence, deliberately, for six seconds]

172
00:11:27,030 --> 00:11:30,530
A meadow that is not drowned does not die.

173
00:11:30,850 --> 00:11:33,850
It simply becomes an ordinary field,

174
00:11:34,170 --> 00:11:37,753
which is a slower and
less interesting fate.

175
00:11:38,073 --> 00:11:41,573
The carriers silt. The banks slump inward.

176
00:11:41,893 --> 00:11:45,226
The ruled lines go soft and then go out.

177
00:11:45,546 --> 00:11:49,463
<i>1988: the same aerial
shot, and almost no lines.</i>

178
00:11:49,783 --> 00:11:53,366
By 1988 the survey could
find eleven meadows

179
00:11:53,686 --> 00:11:57,269
still working, out of
six hundred and forty.

180
00:11:57,589 --> 00:12:01,089
- Out of how many?
- Six hundred and forty.

181
00:12:01,409 --> 00:12:05,409
Eleven. And two of those
were kept by one family.

182
00:12:05,729 --> 00:12:08,146
The water has to keep moving.

183
00:12:08,466 --> 00:12:11,966
Nobody moved it here
between 1971 and 2005.

184
00:12:12,286 --> 00:12:15,619
What happened in 2005 was not nostalgia.

185
00:12:15,939 --> 00:12:19,356
It was a bird count, and it was very bad.

186
00:12:19,676 --> 00:12:23,093
<i>A snipe, in reeds, refusing to be filmed.</i>

187
00:12:27,293 --> 00:12:30,876
A drowned meadow in
February is soft ground.

188
00:12:31,196 --> 00:12:35,029
Soft ground is a bird
that feeds with its face.

189
00:12:35,349 --> 00:12:39,016
Snipe, lapwing, redshank,
curlew: all of them

190
00:12:39,336 --> 00:12:43,253
push a long bill into
ground that will let them.

191
00:12:43,573 --> 00:12:46,823
An ordinary field in February will not.

192
00:12:47,143 --> 00:12:51,560
- It is not the water they want.
- It is the softness.

193
00:12:51,880 --> 00:12:56,380
- And the water makes the softness.
- The moving water.

194
00:12:56,700 --> 00:13:00,450
The county lost ninety
per cent of its lapwing

195
00:13:00,770 --> 00:13:05,270
between 1971 and 2004. The
dates are not a coincidence.

196
00:13:05,590 --> 00:13:09,090
[lapwing, two of them, over the top field]

197
00:13:09,990 --> 00:13:13,240
Alban Pryce came to the valley in 2005,

198
00:13:13,560 --> 00:13:16,977
with a grant, a survey and no idea at all

199
00:13:17,297 --> 00:13:20,464
how a hatch board is hung in a groove.

200
00:13:20,784 --> 00:13:22,951
- None.
- He had a diagram.

201
00:13:23,271 --> 00:13:27,771
- Was the diagram wrong?
- The diagram was upside down.

202
00:13:28,091 --> 00:13:32,341
This is not a criticism.
Nobody had written it down.

203
00:13:32,661 --> 00:13:36,911
Three hundred years of
practice, and the whole of it

204
00:13:37,231 --> 00:13:41,064
was in four people, and
one of them was ninety.

205
00:13:41,384 --> 00:13:45,134
<i>Alban Pryce, at a hatch,
getting it right now.</i>

206
00:13:45,454 --> 00:13:49,204
What he did first was
the only sensible thing.

207
00:13:49,524 --> 00:13:53,357
He asked Winifred Sallis
to come and watch him.

208
00:13:53,677 --> 00:13:57,427
- And you went?
- I went every day for a year.

209
00:13:57,747 --> 00:14:01,914
- To teach him?
- To stop him drowning the village.

210
00:14:02,234 --> 00:14:05,984
That is a joke, but it
is not entirely a joke.

211
00:14:06,304 --> 00:14:10,137
Two inches of water is
a float. Six is a flood,

212
00:14:10,457 --> 00:14:14,040
and Wraycombe is eleven
feet below the main.

213
00:14:14,360 --> 00:14:17,860
[the Ashe, in spate,
under the road bridge]

214
00:14:18,760 --> 00:14:21,593
The restoration took four winters.

215
00:14:21,913 --> 00:14:25,246
Fourteen hatches, nine miles of carrier,

216
00:14:25,566 --> 00:14:29,066
and one argument about
the rota that lasted

217
00:14:29,386 --> 00:14:32,719
rather longer than the 1712 version did.

218
00:14:33,039 --> 00:14:36,122
- What was the argument about?
- When.

219
00:14:36,442 --> 00:14:39,692
- Of course it was.
- It is always when.

220
00:14:40,012 --> 00:14:43,679
<i>The rota, rewritten,
pinned in the same room.</i>

221
00:14:43,999 --> 00:14:47,249
The meadow floated again in March 2009.

222
00:14:47,569 --> 00:14:51,652
Four people were there.
Three of them were crying,

223
00:14:51,972 --> 00:14:56,055
and the fourth was checking
the fall with a level.

224
00:15:00,255 --> 00:15:03,172
- Which were you?
- I had the level.

225
00:15:03,492 --> 00:15:06,909
[hatch boards, lifted, one after another]

226
00:15:07,809 --> 00:15:10,559
This is the meadow this February.

227
00:15:10,879 --> 00:15:13,629
Twenty acres, two inches, moving.

228
00:15:13,949 --> 00:15:18,282
<i>Aerial: the ruled lines are
back, and they are sharp.</i>

229
00:15:18,602 --> 00:15:21,935
The snipe returned in the second winter.

230
00:15:22,255 --> 00:15:26,588
The lapwing took six, and
came back to the top field,

231
00:15:26,908 --> 00:15:30,741
which is the field the
diagram had upside down.

232
00:15:31,061 --> 00:15:34,478
The grass still starts three weeks early.

233
00:15:34,798 --> 00:15:37,548
Nobody here needs it to any more,

234
00:15:37,868 --> 00:15:41,785
which is a strange thing
to say about a miracle.

235
00:15:42,105 --> 00:15:45,022
- Does that bother you?
- Should it?

236
00:15:45,342 --> 00:15:49,259
- It might bother a farmer.
- I am not a farmer.

237
00:15:49,579 --> 00:15:52,912
- What are you?
- A drowner. I have said.

238
00:15:53,232 --> 00:15:56,899
<i>She walks the main,
checking the fall by eye.</i>

239
00:15:57,219 --> 00:16:00,636
The hay is cut once a year, in late June,

240
00:16:00,956 --> 00:16:04,206
and sold to people who keep old horses.

241
00:16:04,526 --> 00:16:07,359
It does not pay for a church roof.

242
00:16:07,679 --> 00:16:11,429
It pays for the diesel
of the man who cuts it,

243
00:16:11,749 --> 00:16:15,332
and it is, by every
account, remarkable hay.

244
00:16:15,652 --> 00:16:19,652
- What is in it?
- Everything. That is the point.

245
00:16:19,972 --> 00:16:23,889
Forty-one flowering species
in one square metre,

246
00:16:24,209 --> 00:16:28,376
counted last June by a
student from the university,

247
00:16:28,696 --> 00:16:32,196
who was not told what she was standing in.

248
00:16:32,516 --> 00:16:36,599
<i>The quadrat, and the count,
written on a wet page.</i>

249
00:16:36,919 --> 00:16:41,002
An ordinary field of the
same size would give six.

250
00:16:41,322 --> 00:16:44,905
- Six.
- And four of the six would be grass.

251
00:16:45,225 --> 00:16:47,642
The water has to keep moving.

252
00:16:47,962 --> 00:16:51,379
It has moved across this ground in winter

253
00:16:51,699 --> 00:16:55,116
for three hundred and twenty-seven years,

254
00:16:55,436 --> 00:16:58,686
with a gap of thirty-four in the middle

255
00:16:59,006 --> 00:17:02,506
that nobody in this film likes to discuss.

256
00:17:02,826 --> 00:17:06,409
[a hatch, dropped, and
the hiss going quiet]

257
00:17:07,309 --> 00:17:11,476
There are now nineteen
working meadows on the Ashe.

258
00:17:11,796 --> 00:17:15,546
There were eleven in the
whole county in 1988.

259
00:17:15,866 --> 00:17:20,199
- So it is going the right way.
- It is going slowly.

260
00:17:20,519 --> 00:17:24,352
- But the right way.
- Slowly is the right way.

261
00:17:28,552 --> 00:17:31,969
A carrier cut in a hurry silts in a year.

262
00:17:32,289 --> 00:17:35,789
A carrier cut properly lasts a generation,

263
00:17:36,109 --> 00:17:39,692
and the man who cut it
will not see it silt.

264
00:17:40,012 --> 00:17:43,679
<i>Tobias Reed, seventy-eight,
cutting properly.</i>

265
00:17:43,999 --> 00:17:47,499
He is stitching a carrier he will not use.

266
00:17:47,819 --> 00:17:51,402
It is for the field
below, which is not his,

267
00:17:51,722 --> 00:17:55,472
and which is farmed by
a man he does not like.

268
00:17:55,792 --> 00:17:59,875
- Then why cut it?
- Because the water goes there.

269
00:18:00,195 --> 00:18:04,695
- That is not an answer.
- It is the only one there is.

270
00:18:05,015 --> 00:18:08,848
Everything downstream is
somebody else's field.

271
00:18:09,168 --> 00:18:13,251
That was true in 1712 and
it is true this morning,

272
00:18:13,571 --> 00:18:17,571
and it is the reason any
of this survived at all.

273
00:18:17,891 --> 00:18:22,141
<i>The valley, from the hill,
with the light coming up.</i>

274
00:18:22,461 --> 00:18:25,128
Winifred Sallis has no children.

275
00:18:25,448 --> 00:18:29,448
Tobias Reed has three,
and none of them stitches.

276
00:18:29,768 --> 00:18:34,518
Alban Pryce has a diagram, and
now it is the right way up,

277
00:18:34,838 --> 00:18:38,171
and he has written the whole of it down.

278
00:18:38,491 --> 00:18:42,574
- Was that hard?
- Getting her to say it was hard.

279
00:18:42,894 --> 00:18:46,311
- Why?
- Because she thinks it is obvious.

280
00:18:46,631 --> 00:18:49,214
- Is it obvious?
- To her it is.

281
00:18:49,534 --> 00:18:52,701
The document runs to ninety-one pages.

282
00:18:53,021 --> 00:18:56,521
It contains every
measurement in this film,

283
00:18:56,841 --> 00:19:00,341
and one sentence that
is not a measurement.

284
00:19:00,661 --> 00:19:05,078
<i>Page one, the epigraph, in
her words and her spelling.</i>

285
00:19:05,398 --> 00:19:07,815
The water has to keep moving.

286
00:19:08,135 --> 00:19:11,968
[the float, at first
light, doing exactly that]

287
00:19:12,868 --> 00:19:16,785
The hatches come up again
at the end of October.

288
00:19:17,105 --> 00:19:20,438
Three people will be there, and a level.

289
00:19:20,758 --> 00:19:25,508
- Will you be there?
- I have been there since I was four.

290
00:19:25,828 --> 00:19:29,328
- Every year?
- Every year there was water.

291
00:19:29,648 --> 00:19:31,981
There has always been water.

292
00:19:32,301 --> 00:19:35,134
It is the moving of it that stops.

293
00:19:35,454 --> 00:19:39,371
<i>The Ashe, running past
the hatches, indifferent.</i>

294
00:19:39,691 --> 00:19:43,691
The river does not care
whether the boards go up.

295
00:19:44,011 --> 00:19:47,011
It ran through the thirty-four years

296
00:19:47,331 --> 00:19:50,914
exactly as it ran through
the three hundred.

297
00:19:51,234 --> 00:19:54,984
Everything else in this
valley was a decision,

298
00:19:59,184 --> 00:20:02,517
made by somebody, in a room, about when.

299
00:20:02,837 --> 00:20:06,504
[boards, going up, four
of them, in sequence]

300
00:20:07,404 --> 00:20:08,804
{\an8}WRAYCOMBE LONG MEADOW, DROWNED SINCE 1698
